[x] Evaluate existing agent runners before extracting our own =evaluate-agent-runners $Before extracting the sandbox into its own tool, check whether an existing runner already does this better. Requirements: rootless podman on our own hardware, no proprietary cloud; headless unattended runs; several harnesses, at least Claude Code and pi; output only as git commits, no credentials in the container; git submodules; OpenTelemetry; spend caps. Candidates include Dagger's container-use. What is ours regardless: bottom-up submodule landing, the stopping-is-good outcome semantics, standard telemetry attributes. Outcome: adopt, build on top of, or extract ours, recorded here with the reasons.  FINDINGS 2026-09-25, recommendation pending the human's call: extract ours, borrowing ideas. Dagger container-use, Apache 2.0, marked experimental: the agent runs on the host and calls the container through MCP tools, so the harness keeps its own host tools and credentials; isolation is weaker than ours, where the whole harness is inside. obra/packnplay, MIT: closest in shape, the agent runs inside, podman works via DOCKER_CMD=podman, and it reads devcontainer config; but it mounts the main repo .git read-write, which is the hook and config escape path we rejected for worktrees, mounts git credentials and ssh keys by default, is interactive-first, and says nothing of submodules. Built-in harness sandboxes, Claude Code bubblewrap and Codex Landlock, are per-harness process confinement, not a shared runner. Surveyed via the 2026-05 coding-agent-sandboxes list by wincent: nothing found combines headless queue runs, stopping-is-good outcomes, bottom-up submodule landing and standard telemetry. Worth borrowing: podman's libkrun runtime, podman run --runtime krun, gives microVM isolation with the same interface, an upgrade path without leaving podman.  DECIDED 2026-09-25 by the human: extract ours, after sandbox-driver-split. It is good enough to use now, and nothing surveyed fits the requirements.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T16:08 %2026-09-25T16:13 #01a0dad3-fe6c-7f11-b7a1-aad22dff5e11
[x] Summarize running and finished runs on demand =sandbox-status $scripts/agent-status, for all runs or one id: state, elapsed, idle seconds since the last transcript event, event count, and the last few tool calls. Stuck means the container is running and idle for more than N minutes; the transcript's modification time is the heartbeat, so nothing hooks into the harness. Each harness adapter owns summarizing its own event stream, since claude stream-json and pi json mode differ; the status command stays generic. This is the on-demand bridge: the lasting answer is telemetry, sandbox-telemetry, which serves the same questions from the collector across sandbox and interactive sessions and every exporting harness. Found 2026-09-25: the only live signal was podman ps, while the transcript already held the whole story.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T16:26 %2026-09-25T16:30 #01a0dae3-f79f-7601-8d06-00c5b01ae657
[x] Move the sandbox image to a root Containerfile =sandbox-root-containerfile $podman build finds a root Containerfile with no flags, so the convention is simply the repo's default Containerfile; any repo that has one works with the sandbox. Move agents/Containerfile to the platform root and add a root .containerignore holding a single asterisk: code arrives through the mount, and without it every build ships clearhead-core/target and node_modules as context. One environment for the superproject; per-submodule images plus compose were rejected as ceremony, since scoping a run to one submodule needs only the prompt.  ADDED 2026-09-25: pin the base image by digest, FROM docker.io/library/archlinux@sha256:..., not latest. Changing one package name rebuilt every layer from that day's Arch and broke on unrelated upstream changes: a LuaRocks 3.13.0 crash and npm blocking Claude Code's install script by default. Keep additions in their own layers so they do not invalidate the pacman layer. Also fix the Claude Code install so it works under npm's new default: allow its install script explicitly.  IMPLEMENTED 2026-09-25 by Codex, gpt-5.6-sol, in run 20260925-170315; reviewed by Claude, verdict land; the image build was verified on the host. Its commits were lost to an agent-land bug before harvest and reapplied from the reviewed diff in 2834309.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 %2026-09-25T17:43 #01a0dab3-2d6b-7c92-9bef-3457a5bf4c4e
[x] Snapshot the harness into each run =sandbox-harness-snapshot $agents/ is mounted live into the running container, so editing agents/loop mid-run can corrupt a shell that reads it incrementally. agent-run copies the harness into the run directory and mounts that copy read-only: each run keeps exactly the harness it started with, which also makes runs reproducible.  IMPLEMENTED 2026-09-25 by Codex, gpt-5.6-sol, in run 20260925-170315; reviewed by Claude, verdict land; the image build was verified on the host. Its commits were lost to an agent-land bug before harvest and reapplied from the reviewed diff in 2834309.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 %2026-09-25T17:43 #01a0dab3-2db5-76e3-b433-81d7e9963547
[x] Pin the sandbox image's packages to a dated Arch snapshot =sandbox-dated-snapshot $DECIDED 2026-09-25 with the human: stay on Arch and pin packages to a dated Arch Linux Archive snapshot; revisit only if dated snapshots prove not good enough. Nix, not Debian, is the base to consider then: Debian's Rust, Node, Neovim and tree-sitter are too old here, so half the toolchain would come from upstream installers with a pin each. Why: pinning the base image by digest does not pin packages, because the first layer runs pacman -Syu, which installs whatever Arch ships that day; a cross-vendor review of the root Containerfile found this, and the action that asked only for a digest was the wrong fix. Do: in the root Containerfile, before pacman -Syu, write /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist as Server=https://archive.archlinux.org/repos/2026/09/25/\$repo/os/\$arch, matching the base digest's era; 2026-09-25 is the date whose packages were built and verified on the host. Updating becomes a deliberate change of date. Keep Arch parity with the host: same Rust, Node and tree-sitter. Verify by building on the host, since podman is not available inside the sandbox. Depends on the root Containerfile run landing first. Implemented in fa949a5: the root Containerfile now replaces /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist with the 2026/09/25 Arch Linux Archive URL before pacman -Syu, so package resolution is tied to the host-verified snapshot while retaining Arch toolchain parity. Verified with the full clearhead-core gate and clearhead doctor. The image build remains unverified in this sandbox because podman is unavailable, as expected by the action.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T17:39 %2026-09-26T01:33 #01a0db26-bb79-7a01-bef8-220622a4819e
[x] Land run 20260925-171333 after its Codex review =land-run-171333 $Claude, claude-sonnet-5, implemented charters-default-new in clearhead-core as 2d5efcc: loading, init, jot's path-derived id, the add action hint, the doctor warning and the CLI README. It hit its 6 dollar cap before committing the completion; the orchestrator committed that as 8cf39c8 in the run's clone. Already harvested into platform and clearhead-core as branch agent/20260925-171333. A read-only Codex review was running at handoff; its findings are in ~/agent-runs/20260925-171333/reviews/pi-codex.jsonl, final assistant message_end. Next: read the verdict; if land, scripts/agent-land 20260925-171333 with ~/.local/bin on PATH for check-jsonschema, then push; if findings block, a follow-up work run. Note that sandbox Claude runs use the subscription default model, Sonnet, since no model is set; decide that explicitly.  REVIEW VERDICT, Codex: ### Findings  - **Should-fix** — `crates/clearhead-cli/src/cli/action.rs:100,1337`: after adding the action, `warn_if_charter_is_new` reloads the entire workspace and silently suppresses loading errors. `resolve_acts_file` already loads and then discards the target charter in normal resolution paths. Return the resolved charter metadata alongside the path and emit the reminder from that, avoiding duplicate I/O and ensuring the required notice is not silently lost.  - **Should-fix** — `crates/clearhead-cli/src/cli/action.rs:1355`, `crates/clearhead-core/src/workspace/store/doctor.rs:248`, `crates/clearhead-cli/src/cli/init.rs:130`: generated activation commands interpolate aliases or titles without shell quoting. A charter such as `Someday Work` produces `clearhead update charter Someday Work --state active`, which is not executable as shown. Use a safely quoted selector or stable identifier, and add coverage for names containing spaces or shell metacharacters.  Validation: the full gate passes from a writable archive with `CLEARHEAD_SPEC_DIR=/job/work/specifications`. The read-only review checkout itself cannot run tests that write into their current directory.  **Verdict: land after fixes.** LANDED 2026-09-25: Follow-up in clearhead-core 57e403b and 4f52750 reuses the resolved Charter metadata (including document-only --file targets), prints stable UUID activation selectors, and tests shell-unsafe full titles. Gemini independently reviewed the full branch and final delta, verdict land. The repository-wide landing gate exposed a conformance fixture with an implicit New root; aebc88e explicitly activates that fixture, separately reviewed by Gemini. scripts/agent-land 20260925-171333 then passed scripts/validate-pinned and landed the branch in platform as 9103e2d. No further fixes required.$ !1 ^2026-09-25T17:48 %2026-09-25T18:55 #01a0db2f-1225-7c73-8f43-a8684527d40e
[x] Run sandbox jobs as user Quadlets =sandbox-quadlet-lifecycle $DECIDED 2026-09-25: move per-run container lifecycle and host resource limits from scripts/agent-run podman run flags into rootless Podman Quadlet units owned by systemd --user. Preserve the disposable clone, pinned harness snapshot, secret isolation, Git-only output, harvest-before-land, and work/review boundaries. One run has a uniquely named unit; systemd enforces CPU, memory, tasks/PIDs and a wall-time deadline, with graceful stop and reliable terminal manifest reconciliation for success, failure, timeout, OOM and manual stop. No automatic restart of mutation work. The script may prepare a run, image and unit and wait/inspect status, but must not be the resource supervisor. Keep model-dollar spend enforcement separate: systemd cannot account for USD, so retain the harness/provider budget and report any Pi overshoot honestly. Update agent-status for unit state rather than only podman ps. The repo root Containerfile remains the image source; verify generated units with host systemd/Quadlet tooling and a bounded host smoke run, plus script tests where practical (podman/systemd are unavailable inside the agent sandbox). Do not implement an interactive prompt/control channel in this action; note what unit identity/logging would make later attachment possible. Stop and record NEEDS DECISION if user Quadlet placement, privilege boundary, or stop/reconciliation semantics cannot be chosen without changing the charter principles. Review by another vendor before landing. IMPLEMENTED 2026-09-26 (commit 3b92da4 in platform): scripts/agent-run now renders a per-run Quadlet unit (agent-<id>.container) into \$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/containers/systemd/ instead of shelling a `podman run` with resource flags, then does `systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user start --wait agent-<id>.service`; systemd's cgroup enforces memory/CPU/tasks-PIDs (\[Service] MemoryMax=16G, CPUQuota=800%, TasksMax=4096) and a new wall-time deadline (RuntimeMaxSec, env AGENT_RUN_DEADLINE_SEC default 21600s), Restart=no so nothing is auto-retried. scripts/agent-stop <id> requests an early graceful stop (TimeoutStopSec, env AGENT_STOP_GRACE_SEC default 60s) and marks the run so reconciliation reports "stopped" rather than an ambiguous signal/failure. Reconciliation (scripts/lib/agent-quadlet.sh: classify_agent_outcome) reads Result/ExecMainCode/ExecMainStatus off the stopped unit for success/failure/signal/oom, but judges a deadline hit by wall time agent-run measures itself around the wait, not by matching Result=timeout: RuntimeMaxSec's expiry is otherwise ambiguous with a clean exit, since a process that exits cleanly on the SIGTERM systemd sends still reads Result=success unless TimeoutStopSec also elapses. oom-kill is trusted directly (systemd sets it independent of OOMPolicy). agent-status now checks unit state (agent_unit_active) instead of `podman ps`.

Decisions made, none requiring a charter change so no NEEDS DECISION: placement is \$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/containers/systemd/, Quadlet's documented path for temporary units (podman-systemd.unit(5)), not the persistent \$XDG_CONFIG_HOME one, so an interrupted cleanup self-clears at reboot; the privilege boundary is unchanged (UserNS=keep-id, same rootless mapping as the old --userns=keep-id); CPU/memory/tasks-PIDs are enforced via \[Service] systemd resource control, not Podman's --memory/--cpus/--pids-limit flags, matching the action's "systemd enforces" and putting all three limits through one mechanism instead of two.

render_agent_unit and classify_agent_outcome are pure functions, tested by scripts/agent-quadlet.test.sh (passes in this sandbox, no podman/systemd needed). Not implemented: an interactive attach/control channel (out of scope here) — noted in agent-run's header that the unit's identity (agent-<id>.service) and journald's per-unit log (`journalctl --user -u agent-<id>.service`) are what a later attach would key off.

NOT DONE / NEEDS HOST VERIFICATION before landing: podman and a systemd --user session are both unavailable inside this sandbox (confirmed: no D-Bus, "System has not been booted with systemd as init system"), so nothing here exercises the real Quadlet generator, daemon-reload, an actual container start, or provokes OOM/timeout/manual-stop for real. The action anticipated this ("verify generated units with host systemd/Quadlet tooling and a bounded host smoke run... podman/systemd are unavailable inside the agent sandbox"); that verification has to happen on the NUC before this lands. Key facts backing the design were checked against docs.podman.io's podman-systemd.unit(5) (Memory=/PidsLimit=/Secret=/UserNS=/Volume= keys, and that \[Service]/\[Install] sections pass through to systemd unmodified) rather than assumed from memory, but the generator's actual output on this host is still unverified.

gate: scripts/gate.sh passed in clearhead-core (untouched by this change; run as a baseline check). clearhead doctor: unchanged from baseline (0 violations, the same 3 pre-existing warnings on unrelated charters). No new dependency. HOST FOLLOW-UP 2026-09-25 (before landing): systemd 262/Podman 6.1.2 Quadlet generator dry-run accepted the rendered unit. Bounded rootless host units exercised success, exit failure, RuntimeMaxSec timeout, requested stop, and OOM. Findings corrected: ExecMainCode is numeric (a very short successful unit may already report 0); use Result=timeout rather than launcher elapsed time, which includes startup; manual stop during stop grace may report Result=timeout and is recorded as stopped:timeout; MemoryMax alone permitted swap, so MemorySwapMax=0 now makes the bound meaningful. A no-model scripts/agent-run smoke completed with manifest outcome finished and cleaned its unit/container; earlier attempts exposed an unset optional environment variable under set -e, now fixed with preparation-failure reconciliation and durable build.log. Arbitrary action names are passed through /job/action-target to preserve one argument in Quadlet Exec. Review the final delta before landing. RECOVERY ADDENDUM 2026-09-25: Killing the foreground systemctl --wait caller does not stop its Quadlet; while active it can misleadingly expose Result=success, ExecMainCode=0. The launcher now refuses to finalize or remove an active unit. scripts/agent-reconcile <id> independently finalizes a stopped run, preserving the unit if properties are unavailable; agent-status flags RECONCILE instead of pretending it finished. A bounded host test verified manual reconciliation, idempotence and cleanup, and the no-model end-to-end launcher smoke passed again. After a host launcher crash reconciliation is recoverable but manual, not automatically scheduled; interactive control and automatic reaping remain follow-up work.$ !1 ^2026-09-25T21:54 %2026-09-25T22:37 #01a0dc10-d7f1-7cb2-bcc5-9705c119a071
[x] Make the queue loop an orchestrator over work runs =sandbox-driver-split $DECIDED 2026-09-25: orchestration is policy and sits outside the run primitives. Today it is done by the human, or by an interactive agent reading run manifests; the queue loop becomes one more orchestrator, for unattended nights, that starts work runs, not part of the sandbox. Dependency direction: the sandbox runner knows git, podman and harnesses, never ClearHead; the loop, its prompt template and its completed, blocked or unfinished classification live on the ClearHead side and call the runner. The runner's manifest records exit, cost, closing message and commits, never actions. Do after sandbox-root-containerfile and alongside the review-run work. LEARNED from runs 20260925-183142 and 20260925-215528: the generic runner still records action identifiers and snapshots a ClearHead-specific agents/loop; move action lookup, queue policy, prompt, completion/blocked/unfinished classification, and plan-proposal materialization to the ClearHead driver. The sandbox should accept generic run kind, prompt/artifact paths, harness and limits, and emit only run/commit/transcript facts. Preserve Quadlet isolation and the host verification flow.  NEW CONSUMER 2026-09-29 with the human: the runner is a harness-neutral subagent system that any interactive agent (Claude Code, pi, Codex) calls, not only the night loop or a human. CLI-first, since every harness has a shell; an MCP server, if ever, is a thin wrapper over the CLI. So a start must not block: it returns the run id at once, and status and result return the manifest as JSON, which is the contract every harness reads (today agent-run waits on systemctl start --wait and callers must background it). Model choice lives in agents/models.env (2ce0daf), overridable per launch and recorded in the manifest; it moves with the runner.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 %2026-09-30T01:50 #01a0dab3-2d95-7d53-a65e-cb88d090e1bd
[x] Gate an integration candidate before advancing main =sandbox-premerge-gate $Observed 2026-09-25: scripts/agent-land merged clearhead-core and platform before scripts/validate-pinned discovered a spec-conformance fixture failure, leaving both local main branches advanced despite exit 1. Make landing preflight the candidate in an isolated clean integration checkout/worktree with correct pinned submodules and reproducible gate dependencies, then advance the real branches bottom-up only after review and the gate pass. On failure preserve candidate branches, manifest and evidence, without a partially landed main. Preserve existing harvest-before-delete guard and report exact repo/ref/merge state. Do not claim cross-repo atomicity if a later merge or push fails; provide explicit recovery. Tests: failing gate leaves each real main unchanged; passing gate lands correct submodule pins and cleanup; fresh-clone npm dependency preparation (including approved tree-sitter-cli installer) is explicit rather than hidden in the developer checkout. DONE 2026-09-30 (platform 03af378, d29eba5): agent-land builds the merge in \$AGENT_RUNS/<id>/candidate, runs the repo's .sandbox/gate there in the sandbox image built from the candidate's Containerfile (after .sandbox/setup), and fast-forwards the real branches bottom-up only on green; a branch that moved since the candidate was built stops before anything advances; a partial fast-forward names what advanced and a rerun skips repos already at the candidate. Evidence stays in the workspace (gate.log, manifest .gate). Dependency preparation is explicit: tree-sitter-actions commits its npm allowScripts, the image adds topiary and graphviz, validate-pinned builds the pinned CLI and pinned parser. Tests: scripts/agent-land.test.sh (red gate, moved branch, green landing with pin and cleanup, unharvested refusal, untouched repo). First real landing: workspace 20260930-101709, gate 53s.$ !1 ^2026-09-25T22:45 %2026-09-30T10:24 #01a0dc3f-7483-7122-ba65-a73ece66b115
[x] Add review runs and harness selection =sandbox-cross-vendor-review $RESOLVED by option A (orchestrator, 2026-09-30). Previous NEEDS DECISION: What transcript evidence counts as a changed file actually read, especially for shell-based review, and is conservative undercounting acceptable?
Evidence: agents/session:81-97 retains pi message_end events and Claude stream-json; scripts/agent-result:43-49 currently returns records without a coverage metric. Both harnesses allow shell tools. A shell argument mentioning a file (grep, git diff, cat, a pipeline, or a command that fails) is not proof its contents reached the reviewer; scanning command strings would let the model inflate the required metric. Counting only successful structured file-read tool results is auditable but excludes shell-based reads and repository diffs, including the grep-heavy review that motivated this action. Partial reads and deleted files also need a stated coverage meaning.
Options: (A) count only successful structured read results, label the metric instrumented-read coverage and explicitly accept undercounting; (B) add host-controlled read instrumentation for shell/diff reads, a scope expansion requiring a defined evidence contract; (C) report tool-observed file access as a heuristic with limitations, relaxing the actually-read requirement. Recommend A if a conservative lower bound is the goal, B if coverage must include shell reviews. No implementation changes made; stopped at this decision point under the Work run rules. Original decision follows unchanged.

DECIDED 2026-09-25 with the human: work and review are separate run kinds, never bundled; deciding which to run is orchestration, kept outside both. A work run takes an action or prompt and mounts its clone read-write; its output is commits and a closing message. A review run takes a finished run's branch, refs/agent/base to agent/id, mounts that clone read-only so report-without-fixing is enforced, and writes findings to its manifest; no commits. Every run picks its harness, claude or pi, through an adapter taking a prompt file and a transcript path and reporting result and cost; a review usually uses the other vendor. The work prompt drops its self-review step, and the runbook's review gate moves to between a work run and landing. pi: headless via pi -p --mode json, final answer from the last message_end event, cost summed from usage.cost; image pins pi 0.85.1; the sandbox's own ChatGPT/Codex login lives in the agent-pi volume, OpenRouter as fallback via the openrouter_token secret; the subscription quota is shared with interactive work. agent-run shares the host pi settings minus pi-background-tasks, remote-pi and pi-memory, and the host agents directory, read-only.  LESSONS 2026-09-25 from a manual pi work run and two pi reviews, for the adapter: pi exits 0 even when its session ends in error, so read the last assistant message_end stopReason; the final text is in message_end content; cost sums message_end usage.cost.total. Take the outcome from the action's real state in read actions, which includes completed actions; an action missing from it is not completed. Exclude pi-mcp-adapter too, or pi loads the repo's own .mcp.json. The adapter must harvest like agent-run: an unharvested manual run lost its commits to agent-land, now guarded. Codex once hit WebSocket 1011 and then an invalid-key error on retry; a rerun succeeded. LEARNED: independent patch-only no-tool reviews missed numeric systemd properties, a missing-anchor reminder, and launcher-interruption behavior; host smoke plus an actual read-only branch checkout caught these. Make review a first-class run kind with the finished branch mounted read-only and access to the action decision/specs, full diff and validation evidence. Emit a durable verdict and findings; do not mutate reviewed files, choose a different vendor from the worker, and require explicit reconciliation of blocking findings before landing. NARROWED 2026-09-30 with the human, after reading a review transcript: the reviewer did real work (15 tool calls, every caller grepped) but its closing message dropped the evidence, so 'land, no findings' could not be told apart from 'did not check'. What is left: the Review run section asks for a closing fenced JSON block {coverage: \[{check, how}], findings: \[{severity, file, line, what, why}], for_human: \[where judgment is needed], verdict}; the host parses it into the session record as review; agent-result adds a transcript-derived coverage count (changed files the session actually read, against the files the change touched), which the model cannot inflate; agent-harvest prints coverage, findings and for_human. A verdict informs landing and never gates it: agent-land prints unreconciled blocking findings and the human decides. Blocking findings go to a fixer session in the same workspace. DECISION (orchestrator, 2026-09-30): option A. Count only changed files opened in full by successful structured file-read tool results (pi read, Claude Code Read), for both transcript formats. Report opened_in_full: {read: \[...], changed: N} as a labeled lower bound, never reviewed. Shell/diff reads are excluded and belong in reviewer coverage. B host instrumentation and C shell-string parsing rejected. Implementing the remaining NARROWED scope. IMPLEMENTED 2026-09-30: Review run closes with validated fenced JSON coverage/findings/for_human/verdict. Host finalization persists the report and a transcript-derived opened_in_full lower bound; agent-result serves it, harvest prints it, land prints unreconciled blocking findings without gating. Human reconciliation stays in the session record and survives later reads. Read-only launch captures changed paths across all repos (including deleted paths, both rename sides, and submodule pins), independent of later fixer sessions. jq, already installed, parses JSON and the output formats; no new dependency. Conservative counting: successful pi read events or Claude Read tool-use/result pairs, default whole-file requests, textual nontruncated results only; unknown/missing evidence yields null, not a fabricated zero. Claude results at the default 2000-line ceiling are excluded. Tests cover both harnesses, partial/failed/truncated/shell reads, immutable denominator, deleted/renamed paths, report parsing, reconciliation persistence and evidence surviving removal of its inputs. Verified: agent-review, agent-runs and agent-quadlet tests, shell syntax and diff checks, core gate green; doctor remains at baseline 3 warnings/0 violations. LSP checks unavailable/unsupported. Container-backed agent-land test cannot run: podman is absent per sandbox rules. No image changes. Implementation commit: c3aa543 (Persist structured review reports and conservative read coverage). Remaining NARROWED scope is done; separate cross-vendor review is an orchestrator step before landing, not performed by this worker.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 %2026-09-30T20:52 #01a0dab3-2e1a-79c2-a788-6901734ddadf
[x] Add a fix run kind that turns a review into a fixer session $DECIDED 2026-09-30 with the human: runs chain by kind inside one workspace (work, review, fix) and run in parallel across workspaces. Fix is the missing kind: today the orchestrator hand-writes every fixer prompt. Runner-level and harness-neutral, no ClearHead: scripts/agent-fix <workspace>\[/<n>] \[--harness claude|pi] \[--model id] \[--note <file|text>] \[--wait] starts a writer session in that workspace (through scripts/agent-run --in) whose prompt is built from the review of read-only session n (default: the latest read-only session with a parsed review): the verdict, every blocking and should-fix finding verbatim as JSON (nits only if --nits), and the --note text, which carries the human's or orchestrator's answers to the review's for_human points and any decisions. Refuses when that session has no review or it is unparsed, and when no finding is blocking or should-fix and there is no note. Prints the session ref on stdout like agent-run. Runbook: a Fix run section: address each finding in the prompt or say why not, stay inside the findings and the note, commit, and end with a fenced json block {fixed: \[finding what], declined: \[{what, why}]}; a fix does not reconcile a finding, the human does. Test with fixture records under scripts/agent-fix.test.sh (prompt content, refusals), no podman. IMPLEMENTED 2026-09-30 in fe3cd24: scripts/agent-fix launches a writer through agent-run --in, selecting the latest numerically ordered read-only parsed review or an explicit session. Reuses workspace_json and the shared jq review validator/parser; preserves complete finding objects, includes blocking and should-fix findings (nits with --nits), verdict and file/text note, and forwards harness, model, wait, stdout reference and exit status. Refuses missing/unparsed reviews, writer sessions, invalid references and no actionable findings without a note. Added the Fix run procedure and orchestrator usage. Fixture tests cover prompt JSON, numeric session selection, closing-only records, note-only work, options, launcher status and refusals without podman. agent-fix, agent-review, agent-runs and agent-quadlet shell tests and shell syntax checks passed; clearhead-core full gate passed. Doctor unchanged: 0 violations, 3 existing new-charter-open-actions warnings. LSP checks unavailable for shell and Markdown; syntax and executable tests provide validation. No new dependencies; no Containerfile change; actual host/container launch is unverified, fixture launcher only.$ !1 ^2026-09-30T14:32 %2026-09-30T21:37 #01a0f43c-0e87-72dc-8cef-c03b868d282c
[x] Fix the two findings from the fix-run-kind review $Review by the orchestrator 2026-09-30 of the landed fix run kind (platform 02ac590), verdict land after fixes, fixed forward. Should-fix, docs/overnight-runbook.md Fix run section: it restates the every-run rules (gate, dependencies, commit trailer), including the old gate wording that 0535c87 corrected; keep only what is specific to a fix run and point at the every-run rules for the rest. Should-fix, scripts/agent-fix: a review can only come from a read-only session's record, but the human chose the orchestrator as reviewer, and the orchestrator's reviews are not sessions; accept --review <file> holding a review in the same JSON shape (validated with valid_review_report), used instead of a session's review, and cover it in scripts/agent-fix.test.sh. Implemented in platform commit 4edbd08: Fix run now references the every-run and Work requirements rather than restating stale gate/dependency/provenance rules. agent-fix accepts --review <file>, validates exactly one JSON report with the existing valid_review_report, and uses it instead of session selection. Tests cover relative paths, verbatim findings, nits and note authorization, malformed/missing/empty/multiple reports, explicit session override, and workspaces without parsed reviews. sh scripts/agent-fix.test.sh and shell syntax/diff checks passed; clearhead-core sh scripts/gate.sh passed all checks. Doctor remained at 0 violations and the same 3 pre-existing warnings. LSP checks unavailable for these shell/Markdown files; no new dependencies.$ !1 ^2026-09-30T14:50 %2026-09-30T21:55 #01a0f44c-58b0-7733-a522-885e23d646f6
[x] Record the human's verdict on each landing $DECIDED 2026-09-30: the feedback loop needs the human's call next to the reviewer's, or no reviewer can be calibrated. Per landing, store in the workspace manifest {agrees_with_review, overruled: \[finding], missed: \[what the human found that the review did not], note}. One line per landing; the orchestrator may record it on the human's word. This is the data that lets usage grow: miss rate and false-alarm rate per reviewer and per kind of change decide where human review can get lighter. After sandbox-cross-vendor-review. Shape, 2026-09-30: scripts/agent-verdict <workspace> \[--agree] \[--overrule <text>]... \[--missed <text>]... \[--note <text>] writes {agrees_with_review, overruled, missed, note, at} as the manifest's human_verdict; repeatable flags, no interactive prompts, so the orchestrator can record it on the human's word. agent-land prints a reminder when a landed workspace has a review but no human_verdict. Runbook: one line in For the orchestrator. IMPLEMENTED in platform c8bbafc: scripts/agent-verdict records human_verdict with agrees_with_review (false unless --agree), repeatable overruled and missed text arrays, note and timestamp. It atomically replaces the verdict while preserving other manifest facts, works after landing, and refuses invalid workspace references and options. agent-land prints an advisory after a successful landing when the already-loaded workspace has a review but no human verdict; no second workspace read. Added the single orchestrator runbook line. Fixture tests cover repeated flags, quoting/newlines, defaults, replacement, preservation, refusals, malformed JSON without clobbering, and reminders for separate and legacy inline review records. agent-verdict, agent-fix, agent-review, agent-runs and agent-quadlet tests, shell syntax and diff checks passed; clearhead-core full gate passed. Doctor unchanged: 0 violations and 3 existing warnings. LSP checks unavailable for shell/Markdown. 